                        Emu68DeviceTree 1.0.2
                        =====================

                  Display the PiStorm/Emu68 Device Tree
                    Written by Philippe CARPENTIER


WHAT IT IS
==========

Emu68DeviceTree is a MUI browser for the Device Tree published by Emu68
on PiStorm boards. That tool may help the lowlevel PiStorm/Emu68 developers.

Browse the whole tree, search it by name, inspect a property's raw bytes
and decoded value, and save any node (or the whole tree) to a plain text
file. A command line mode (SEARCH=, NODE=) skips the GUI entirely and
writes straight to standard output, for remote sessions and scripting.


REQUIREMENTS
============

  - AmigaOS 3.0 or better. Nothing newer than V36 is called.

  - A machine exposing the devicetree.resource.
    In practice a PiStorm board running Emu68.

  - MUI 3.8 or better.
    http://aminet.net/package/util/libs/mui38usr

  - MCC_NList, MCC_NListview, MCC_NListtree.
    http://aminet.net/package/dev/mui/MCC_NList-0.128
    
  - MCC_HexEdit, by Miloslaw Smyk.
    http://aminet.net/package/dev/mui/MCC_HexEdit


INSTALLATION
============

Copy Emu68DeviceTree wherever you like. The program needs no assign and no
configuration file. Its settings are the MUI ones, reachable from the
Project menu. A command line argument is optional, see COMMAND LINE below.


COMMAND LINE
============

Give the program SEARCH= or NODE= and it never opens a window, never even
touches MUI: it writes to standard output and quits, so the result can be
read directly or redirected to a file.

  Emu68DeviceTree SEARCH=watchdog
  Emu68DeviceTree NODE="/soc/watchdog@7e100000/" >RAM:watchdog.txt

SEARCH= filters the tree exactly as the search gadget would (case
insensitive, with full paths), keeping matches' parents for context.
NODE= then dumps that one node (or property) whole, in full, exactly like
selecting it in the tree and using "Save node as...".

HELP prints a short summary of this and quits. Typing "Emu68DeviceTree ?"
shows the SEARCH/K,NODE/K,HELP/S template first, which is where HELP
comes from.


